      {
        "id": "p47-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of any points of inflection.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,3)$ and $(2,19)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}12x(2-x)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }2\\\\y(0)&=3\\\\y(2)&=3+4(8)-16\\\\y(2)&=19.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x<0$, $y''<0$; for $0<x<2$, $y''>0$; and for $x>2$, $y''<0$. Concavity changes at both values, so the points of inflection are $(0,3)$ and $(2,19)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p47-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of the function, indicating all important features. Give any approximate intercepts to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Downward-ended quartic with horizontal inflection $(0,3)$, inflection $(2,19)$ and maximum $(3,30)$. Intercepts: $(0,3)$, approximately $(-0.85,0)$ and $(4.05,0)$.",
          "worked": "The domain is $\\mathbb R$. The function is neither odd nor even and has no asymptotes.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $(0,3)$. Solving $3+4x^3-x^4=0$ numerically gives $x\\approx-0.85$ and $x\\approx4.05$.\n\nThe curve increases for $x<3$, apart from its horizontal tangent at $x=0$, and decreases for $x>3$. It is concave down for $x<0$, concave up for $0<x<2$ and concave down for $x>2$. Mark the horizontal inflection $(0,3)$, inflection $(2,19)$ and maximum $(3,30)$. Since the leading term is $-x^4$, $y\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p47-q10-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
